-- postpartum week 1: Refilling what birth asked of you
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   1,
   NULL,
   'Phase 1 of 6 — Early healing',
   'Refilling what birth asked of you',
   '"This week is about replenishing, not perfecting."',
   'Birth involves blood loss, and the amount varies depending on the birth and the individual. Your body now needs time, food, and any prescribed supplements to rebuild. If your care team has asked you to continue an iron-folic-acid tablet, keep taking it as directed. Iron needs change after pregnancy, but your personal need depends on your own blood loss, your iron levels, whether you''re breastfeeding, and what your care team has prescribed — food supports recovery, it does not replace a prescribed supplement. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: If you eat chicken or fish, these provide some readily absorbed heme iron. Eggs also contribute iron and useful protein, while dal, beans, ragi, sesame, and leafy vegetables provide plant-based iron. A gentle note for this week: This first week is not about getting everything right. If all you can manage some days is toast, or whatever someone hands you, that''s genuinely fine — eating enough, staying hydrated, and feeling steady enough to care for yourself and your baby matter far more than following this chart exactly. Skip anything that doesn''t appeal to you, and choose whatever actually helps you feel okay. Your comfort and mental wellbeing come first. If your appetite or digestion feels unsettled: If your appetite or digestion feels unsettled — from stitches, a caesarean incision, nausea, constipation, or simply not feeling like yourself — choose a smaller portion, or substitute soft moong dal, mashed dal-rice, curd rice, a simple soup, or plain porridge, or another familiar food you tolerate comfortably. Fluids and gentle fibre (fruit, cooked vegetables, whole grains) can help too, introduced gradually rather than all at once. Each day below offers two options. Some days contrast a vegetarian and a non-vegetarian dish; other days offer two different vegetarian dishes. Choose whichever suits you today — there''s no ''right'' path between A and B. If you are managing a health condition - Diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: Choose whole fruit more often than juice; keep laddus, jaggery, and dates as optional small additions rather than iron treatments. Pair carbohydrates with dal, eggs, curd, paneer, nuts, chicken, or fish. Continue your prescribed monitoring plan. - Thyroid medication: Follow the timing advised by your clinician or pharmacist; iron and calcium supplements are commonly kept at least four hours apart from levothyroxine. - High blood pressure or previous pre-eclampsia: Continue prescribed medicines and monitoring. A severe or persistent headache, vision changes, upper-abdominal pain, breathlessness, or sudden swelling needs urgent medical help.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today''s meals lean on familiar, iron-friendly staples — dal, leafy greens, and a wedge of lemon alongside them. There''s no need to get this exactly right; whatever you can manage today is enough.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal khichdi with a spoon of ghee.", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal khichdi with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Palak (spinach) dal with soft-cooked rice and a wedge of lemon.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-spinach curry (cooked through) with soft-cooked rice and a wedge of lemon.", "nourishment_break_a": "Warm milk with a small handful of almonds.", "nourishment_break_b": "Unsweetened ragi porridge — add fruit or a little jaggery only if it suits your glucose-management plan.", "dinner_a": "Lentil-and-vegetable soup with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Palak-and-paneer curry with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "Whatever feels easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Where iron naturally shows up", "notes": "Iron comes in two forms. Heme iron — from chicken and fish — is absorbed efficiently by your body. Non-heme iron is found in eggs and foods such as dal, beans, leafy vegetables, ragi, and sesame; it''s absorbed less efficiently on its own, but pairing it with a vitamin-C-rich food in the same meal (lemon, guava, amla, orange, or tomato) genuinely helps your body take up more of it, a pairing covered in more depth earlier in this series.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a coconut chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with a fully cooked egg curry.", "lunch_a": "Rajma (kidney bean) curry with rice and a wedge of lemon.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-rajma curry (cooked through) with rice and a wedge of lemon.", "nourishment_break_a": "Til (sesame) and jaggery laddu — one small piece, optional.", "nourishment_break_b": "A whole orange or guava with roasted chana.", "dinner_a": "Chana (chickpea) and spinach curry with soft roti and a tomato-onion salad.", "dinner_b": "Soy-and-spinach curry with soft roti and a tomato-onion salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Continuing your prescribed iron-folic-acid tablet", "notes": "India''s national programme includes continued iron-folic-acid supplementation after birth. Your own dose and duration may differ depending on your blood loss, anaemia, test results, and the instructions you received at discharge. Take only the dose advised for you — do not add another iron supplement or double the dose unless your clinician has specifically told you to. If nausea, constipation, forgetfulness, or the demands of caring for your baby make the tablet difficult to manage, tell your care team. They may be able to adjust the timing or formulation. Do not change the dose or stop it permanently without checking with them. Store your iron tablets safely out of reach of children. Iron tablets were historically a leading cause of accidental childhood poisoning, partly because they can look like candy to a young child — modern packaging has made this much rarer, but keeping them out of sight and reach in a home with older siblings around is still the standard, sensible precaution.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a piece of fruit.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a fully cooked egg bhurji.", "lunch_a": "Sambar with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable curry (cooked through) with rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana with a piece of fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with fruit.", "dinner_a": "Methi (fenugreek) and moong dal with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-methi curry with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "When to check in with your care team", "notes": "Tiredness is common during early recovery, but not every symptom should automatically be blamed on broken sleep. Pay attention to anything severe, worsening, or noticeably different from what feels manageable for you. Get help right away if: you are soaking through one or more pads in an hour, passing clots bigger than an egg, experiencing heavy or gushing bleeding, fainting, chest pain, or difficulty breathing. Do not wait for a routine appointment — get urgent medical help now. Also tell your care team if: ongoing dizziness, unusual breathlessness, a racing heartbeat, extreme weakness, or persistent fatigue concerns you — they can decide whether you need a blood test.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with milk.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia porridge with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Kala chana (black chickpea) curry with rice and a wedge of lemon.", "lunch_b": "Soy-and-kala-chana curry with rice and a wedge of lemon.", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of warm milk with a handful of almonds.", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates stuffed with a few walnuts.", "dinner_a": "Mixed-vegetable and moong-dal curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Lower-mercury fish curry (cooked through) with soft rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Easy to prepare, easy to eat", "notes": "This week isn''t the week to cook elaborate meals, and it doesn''t need to be. If family or a helper is preparing food for you, simple, familiar dishes — khichdi, dal, curd rice — are entirely sufficient, and traditional postpartum foods many Indian families already prepare (like a gond, or edible-gum, laddu, or a til-jaggery sweet) are reasonable, calorie-dense additions alongside everything else this week, though none of them replace a genuinely varied plate or your prescribed supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with sambar.", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with a mint-coriander chutney.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable khichdi with a side of curd.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable khichdi (cooked through) with a side of curd.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with a piece of fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Gond (edible gum) laddu — one small piece.", "dinner_a": "Lauki (bottle gourd) and chana dal curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-lauki curry with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Coming and going", "notes": "Follow-up appointments, visits, or simply a day when nobody''s had a chance to cook yet all call for something that doesn''t need much preparation. Roasted nuts, chana, and fruit travel well without refrigeration; carry a cooked egg or paneer item in an insulated bag with an ice pack if you''re bringing it from home.", "breakfast_a": "Whole-grain toast with peanut butter and a banana.", "breakfast_b": "Whole-grain toast with a small bowl of lightly seasoned chana and a banana.", "lunch_a": "A freshly prepared hot whole-wheat paneer-and-vegetable wrap.", "lunch_b": "A freshly prepared hot whole-wheat fish-and-vegetable wrap (lower-mercury fish, cooked through).", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of buttermilk with roasted chana.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted-chana-and-almond trail mix.", "dinner_a": "A hot dal-and-vegetable soup with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "A hot soy-and-vegetable soup with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A week spent gently refilling what birth asked of you — one meal, one supportive choice, one small step at a time — is worth a quiet look back, without any pressure to have done it perfectly. You just did something enormous. Refuelling yourself isn''t indulgent — it''s part of how you heal.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'What has actually felt manageable to eat this week? Was there a moment you needed to lean on someone else for a meal, and how did that feel?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at the energy and fluid needs of early recovery — and, if you are breastfeeding or expressing, what feeding around the clock may ask of you — without turning eating into one more task.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 2: More fuel, not less
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   2,
   NULL,
   'Phase 1 of 6 — Early healing',
   'More fuel, not less',
   '"Your body is asking for more right now — that''s not a problem to fix."',
   'Right now your body is doing demanding work: healing from birth, and — if you''re breastfeeding or expressing — producing milk. Both take real energy and real fluid. This isn''t the week to count calories or restrict food to ''get your body back'' — it''s the week to eat and drink enough to support what your body is actually doing. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Chicken, fish, and eggs provide useful protein in relatively manageable portions. Vegetarian protein sources include dal, beans, soy, paneer, curd, milk, nuts, and seeds. Ghee and oils can add energy when your appetite is low, but they don''t replace a protein source. A gentle note for this week: There''s no single number of calories or glasses of water to hit this week. Let hunger and thirst guide you — eat when you''re hungry, even if that''s more often than before, and don''t worry about matching your usual portions yet. Your comfort and energy matter more than any target. If your appetite feels low instead: If your appetite feels low despite everything your body is doing — from tiredness, pain, or simply not having time — smaller, more frequent plates of familiar food are enough; you don''t need to force large meals. If low appetite continues for more than a few days, or comes with feeling very low or overwhelmed, mention it to your care team. Each day below offers two options. Some days contrast a vegetarian and a non-vegetarian dish; other days offer two different vegetarian dishes. Choose whichever suits you today — there''s no ''right'' path between A and B. If you are managing a health condition - Diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: Spacing meals and snacks evenly across the day — rather than skipping meals when exhausted and eating a large amount later — helps keep your blood sugar steadier. Pair the extra food your body needs right now with protein, fibre, or fat rather than relying on sugary drinks or snacks alone. Continue your prescribed monitoring plan. - Thyroid condition: Both an underactive and an overactive thyroid can affect appetite, energy, and weight in ways that can overlap with normal postpartum tiredness. Keep taking your prescribed treatment, and mention any big, unexplained change in appetite, weight, or energy to your care team rather than assuming it''s just new-parent exhaustion. - High blood pressure or previous pre-eclampsia: Keep taking prescribed medicines and follow any individual advice about fluids or salt. Do not deliberately force large amounts of water, or restrict fluids, because of a general chart like this one. A severe or persistent headache, vision changes, upper-abdominal pain, breathlessness, or sudden swelling needs urgent medical help.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week isn''t about eating less, even if you''re eager to feel more like yourself. Recovering from birth, and — if you''re breastfeeding or expressing — making milk, both take real energy. Feeling hungrier than usual right now is your body asking for fuel, not a lack of discipline.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with a glass of milk.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Chole (chickpea curry) with rice and a cucumber-tomato salad.", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema (cooked through) with rice and a cucumber-tomato salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small handful of peanuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "Hummus with cucumber and carrot sticks.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable kofta curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-green-pea curry with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "Whatever feels easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "How much more fuel your body is actually asking for", "notes": "If you''re breastfeeding or expressing regularly, your body generally needs more energy than it did before pregnancy. The exact amount varies with your body, recovery, activity, appetite, and how much milk you''re producing. You don''t need to calculate it meal by meal — a practical approach is to keep eating regular meals and add another nourishing snack or a slightly larger portion when hunger asks for it. If you''re not breastfeeding, you still need regular, adequate meals while recovering from birth. This is not the time to rush back to restrictive eating.", "breakfast_a": "Besan (chickpea flour) cheela with a mint-coriander chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with a paneer-and-vegetable filling.", "lunch_a": "Masoor dal khichdi with lemon and mixed vegetables.", "lunch_b": "Egg-and-vegetable curry (cooked through) with rice and a wedge of lemon.", "nourishment_break_a": "Dates stuffed with a few walnuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "Boiled sweet potato with a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_a": "Tofu-and-pea curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Mushroom-and-green-pea curry with soft roti — or tofu-and-pea curry if mushrooms aren''t typically used at home.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Staying hydrated without chasing a number", "notes": "Drinking far beyond thirst hasn''t been shown to increase your milk supply. Regular access to fluids supports your own comfort and hydration, while significant dehydration can affect your health and may eventually affect milk production. Drink to thirst, keep water within reach, and drink more when your urine is consistently dark yellow — there''s no need to force large volumes. Get prompt medical advice if: you are passing very little urine, cannot keep fluids down, feel faint or persistently dizzy, have a racing heartbeat, or feel increasingly unwell. These symptoms should not automatically be assumed to be ordinary dehydration.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with milk and a banana.", "breakfast_b": "Oats porridge with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Curd rice with a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with roasted peanuts or chana.", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer cubes with a few walnuts.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable daliya (broken wheat) with moong dal and a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_b": "Lower-mercury fish curry (cooked through) with soft rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "A glass of water, and a familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Feeding around the clock", "notes": "However you''re feeding your baby — direct breastfeeding, expressing, formula feeding, or a combination — newborn feeding rarely follows a tidy schedule. Keeping water and easy food within reach can help during long or closely spaced feeds. If you''re breastfeeding, newborns often feed several times close together, especially during the evening or overnight. This cluster feeding can be normal, and frequent feeding by itself does not automatically mean your milk supply is low. What matters more is whether your baby is swallowing during feeds, producing enough wet nappies, staying appropriately alert, and gaining weight as expected. Get feeding support promptly if: after day 5 of life, your baby has fewer than six noticeably wet nappies in 24 hours, is increasingly sleepy or difficult to wake, feeds poorly, has worsening jaundice, feeding is painful for you, or you''re concerned about weight gain. Food safety: Keep a prepared sandwich or wrap refrigerated until you need it, and discard one that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather).", "breakfast_a": "Peanut butter on whole-grain toast with a banana.", "breakfast_b": "A fully cooked boiled egg with whole-grain toast and a banana.", "lunch_a": "A freshly prepared paneer-and-vegetable sandwich, or one kept refrigerated until needed.", "lunch_b": "A freshly prepared chicken-and-vegetable sandwich, made with fully cooked chicken and kept refrigerated until needed.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana and a piece of fruit — easy to eat one-handed.", "nourishment_break_b": "One small, lightly sweetened date-oat-peanut ball — easy to eat one-handed. If you''re managing blood sugar, choose the roasted chana and fruit option instead.", "dinner_a": "A hot dal-and-vegetable soup you can sip from a mug.", "dinner_b": "A hot soy-and-vegetable soup you can sip from a mug.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When sitting down for a full meal isn''t realistic", "notes": "Some days, three sit-down meals just won''t happen, and that''s alright. Several smaller meals or snacks spread through the day can meet your needs just as well as three big ones — what matters more is eating regularly, rather than skipping meals for long stretches because you''re exhausted or busy.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a coconut chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with a fully cooked chicken sukka (dry-style chicken) on the side.", "lunch_a": "Lemon rice with a mixed-lentil vegetable stew.", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (cooked through) with lemon rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Fruit lassi (no added sugar) with a small handful of nuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "Boiled corn with a squeeze of lemon and a small handful of peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Dal dhokli with a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_b": "Soy chunks and mixed vegetable curry with soft rice.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Let people help you eat, not just hold the baby", "notes": "If family, a helper, or friends want to do something useful, feeding you is one of the most concretely helpful things they can offer. Accepting a home-cooked meal, a batch of frozen parathas, or someone doing the grocery run isn''t a failure to cope — it''s exactly what this stretch of recovery is for.", "breakfast_a": "Methi (fenugreek) paratha with curd.", "breakfast_b": "Methi paratha with paneer bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Kadhi with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Tofu-and-vegetable curry with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "A nut-and-seed mix (almonds, pumpkin seeds, sunflower seeds).", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted makhana (fox nuts) with peanuts, or a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao with masoor dal.", "dinner_b": "Chicken pulao (cooked through) with a side of curd.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A week spent asking your body what it actually needed — more food, more fluids, more rest, more help — is worth a quiet look back. Feeding yourself well right now is not extra — it supports your recovery, your energy, and your ability to move through these demanding early days.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Did there seem to be moments when hunger or thirst was difficult to notice — or when caring for your baby made it hard to respond? What could make food and water easier to reach next week?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at what your body needs to keep repairing and rebuilding as the early weeks of recovery continue.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 3: Protein, the quiet rebuilder
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   3,
   NULL,
   'Phase 1 of 6 — Early healing',
   'Protein, the quiet rebuilder',
   '"Every meal is doing more work than it looks like."',
   'Protein provides some of the building blocks your body uses for tissue repair and, if you''re breastfeeding, milk production. It works as part of a varied, adequately nourishing diet — not alone. This week leans on protein-rich foods spread across your meals, not one enormous protein-heavy plate. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Chicken, fish, and eggs are concentrated protein sources and can help you reach your needs in smaller portions. If you don''t eat them, a varied combination of foods such as dal, beans, soy, paneer, curd, milk, nuts, and seeds can also meet your protein needs well. A gentle note for this week: There''s no single gram target to hit this week. Protein spread naturally across regular meals and a couple of snacks is enough for most mothers — you don''t need to weigh or track anything. Some meals will contain more protein than others, and that''s fine; you don''t need to balance every plate perfectly for the week to support you. If your appetite feels low instead: If eating enough protein feels hard right now — from low appetite, nausea, or simply not having time to cook — small, familiar protein foods (a boiled egg, a glass of milk, a handful of nuts, or a spoon of peanut butter) still count and may feel much more manageable than a full meal. Something simple is worthwhile; it doesn''t have to look like a complete plate every time. If low appetite continues for more than a few days, or comes with feeling very low or overwhelmed, mention it to your care team. Each day below offers two options. Some days contrast a vegetarian and a non-vegetarian dish; other days offer two different vegetarian dishes. Choose whichever suits you today — there''s no ''right'' path between A and B. If you are managing a health condition - Diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: Including a protein-rich food with meals and with many of your snacks may help meals feel more satisfying and support steadier blood glucose. Continue any prescribed monitoring or individual meal guidance — you don''t need to make every snack perfect, and portions like rice, roti, sattu, bananas, or chikki are worth following your own prescribed pairings and amounts for. - Thyroid condition: Soy foods (including soybeans, tofu, and soya chunks) don''t usually need to be avoided, but they can interfere with how well your body absorbs levothyroxine if eaten too close to your dose. Take your medicine consistently as prescribed — usually on an empty stomach — and follow your clinician''s or pharmacist''s advice about the timing of soy-containing foods and any iron or calcium supplements around your medicine. Your own prescription instructions take priority over general timing guidance. - High blood pressure, previous pre-eclampsia, or kidney concerns: Do not deliberately increase or restrict protein based on this general chart. If your kidney function has been affected, or your care team has given you individual dietary guidance, continue that plan.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week is about protein — one of the nutrients your body draws on to rebuild tissue, heal any stitches or incision, and, if you''re breastfeeding, support milk production, alongside everything else a varied diet provides. It shows up throughout the week below: dal, paneer, curd, milk, nuts, and — if you eat them — chicken, fish, and eggs.", "breakfast_a": "Paneer paratha with curd.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Dal makhani with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Chicken malai tikka (cooked through) with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted soybeans, or lightly mashed boiled soybeans/edamame where locally available, with a piece of fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of sattu (roasted gram) drink.", "dinner_a": "Masoor dal and vegetable stew with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Lower-mercury fish curry (cooked through) with soft roti and a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "Whatever feels easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Protein needs after birth", "notes": "If you''re breastfeeding or expressing, your protein needs are higher than before pregnancy — both to support milk production and your own recovery. If you''re not breastfeeding, regular protein-rich meals still support your body as it recovers from birth. Individual needs vary, and you don''t need to calculate or track grams unless your care team has given you a specific target.", "breakfast_a": "Steamed moong-and-matki sprouts chaat with curd and a squeeze of lemon.", "breakfast_b": "Steamed sprouts chaat with a paneer topping.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable-curd rice with a small bowl of moong dal.", "lunch_b": "Lower-mercury fish curry (cooked through) with a whole-wheat roti and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk with a small handful of roasted peanuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A slice of paneer with cucumber sticks.", "dinner_a": "Rajma tikki (patties) with a mint-coriander chutney and a cooked vegetable side.", "dinner_b": "Chicken seekh kebab (cooked through) with a mint-coriander chutney and a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "How protein helps you heal", "notes": "Along with making milk if you''re breastfeeding, your body is also rebuilding tissue right now — healing your uterus, any stitches from a tear or episiotomy, or a caesarean incision. Protein supplies some of the building blocks your body uses to repair tissue and make new skin and muscle. Vitamin-C-rich foods (lemon, guava, amla, orange, tomato) and zinc-rich foods (nuts, seeds, dal, chicken, eggs) — both covered earlier in this series — work alongside protein in that process, so a varied plate does more than protein alone. Get prompt medical advice if: your wound or stitches become increasingly red, swollen, warm, open or separate, develop pus or an unpleasant smell, or if you develop fever or worsening pain.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain toast with a paneer-and-tomato spread.", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain toast with a mashed boiled-egg spread.", "lunch_a": "Matki (moth bean) curry with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a handful of roasted chana.", "nourishment_break_b": "One small piece of peanut chikki (peanut-jaggery brittle), or roasted peanuts if you prefer a less sweet option.", "dinner_a": "Mixed dal (panchmel dal) with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Soy-and-vegetable curry with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Let protein show up across your day", "notes": "Including a protein-rich food at different meals can make it easier to eat enough across the day and can help meals feel more balanced. You don''t need to divide it perfectly or include it every single time — your overall pattern matters more than perfect timing.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed vegetable paratha with curd.", "breakfast_b": "Stuffed vegetable paratha with a fully cooked egg bhurji on the side.", "lunch_a": "Toor dal with rice, a wedge of lemon, and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Fish moilee (coconut fish curry, lower-mercury fish, cooked through) with rice, a wedge of lemon, and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "A handful of roasted almonds and walnuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-paneer stir-fry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Vegetable-and-chicken stir-fry (cooked through) with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Vegetarian protein, done well", "notes": "Vegetarian foods can meet protein needs well when meals include a varied combination of foods such as dal, beans, soy, dairy, nuts, and seeds. You don''t need a special ''superfood'' — variety across the day is enough.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with milk and a spoon of peanut butter.", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge with milk and a small bowl of roasted chana.", "lunch_a": "Dal-and-paneer curry (mixed) with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Soya chunk and vegetable pulao with a side of curd.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana and peanut mix.", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk with a spoon of peanut butter stirred in.", "dinner_a": "Mixed-vegetable khichdi with curd.", "dinner_b": "Mushroom-and-pea curry with soft roti and curd.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Protein you can grab on a hard day", "notes": "On a genuinely hard day, protein doesn''t need to come from a cooked meal. A boiled egg, a glass of milk, a spoon of peanut butter, a handful of nuts, or a tub of curd all count — reach for whatever''s actually within reach. Food safety: Keep a prepared roll refrigerated until you need it, and discard one that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Reheat the khichdi or rice option until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "A glass of roasted-gram (sattu) drink with a banana, prepared with safe water and left unsweetened or lightly sweetened according to preference and any glucose-management plan.", "breakfast_b": "A fully cooked boiled egg with a banana.", "lunch_a": "A pre-made paneer-and-vegetable roll, or one kept refrigerated until needed.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken-and-vegetable roll, made with fully cooked chicken and kept refrigerated until needed.", "nourishment_break_a": "Peanuts and roasted chana mix — easy to eat one-handed.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small tub of curd with a spoon of roasted flaxseed — easy to eat one-handed.", "dinner_a": "A reheated bowl of thick moong-dal and vegetable khichdi with curd.", "dinner_b": "A reheated bowl of rajma-and-vegetable rice.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "You''ve spent three weeks now caring for a body that''s healing and carrying an enormous amount — physically and emotionally. That''s real work, even on the days it didn''t feel like anything special happened. Feeding yourself well is quietly part of how all of this healing happens — even on the days it''s just toast and whatever''s easiest.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Which protein-rich foods felt easiest to reach for this week — and which ones would you want to have on hand more often?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday part of eating well after birth.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
