-- postpartum week 4: Keeping things moving, gently
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   4,
   NULL,
   'Phase 1 of 6 — Early healing',
   'Keeping things moving, gently',
   '"Soft and regular beats forceful and rare."',
   'By now, several ordinary things may still be slowing your digestion: an iron supplement, if one has been prescribed; certain pain medicines, particularly opioid medicines; hormonal changes; moving around less than usual; and simply not drinking enough. Fibre and fluid work together to keep stool soft, which makes bowel movements more comfortable and reduces how much you need to strain. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Meat, fish, and eggs don''t contain fibre — it comes only from plant foods: fruit, vegetables, whole grains, dal, beans, nuts, and seeds. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs, they''re still useful for protein and other nutrients in this week''s meals, just not for fibre itself. A gentle note for this week: You don''t need to choose every high-fibre option in one day. If your usual diet is lower in fibre, begin with one or two gentle additions, and increase gradually as your digestion tolerates it. Keep drinks within reach and sip regularly through the day — fibre without enough fluid can make constipation worse, not better — and follow your clinician''s advice if you''ve been given a fluid restriction. If fibre makes bloating or discomfort worse, ease off the sudden additions — more fibre isn''t always the answer to every kind of abdominal discomfort, and it''s worth mentioning ongoing symptoms to your care team. If a full plate feels like too much: If a full plate of vegetables or whole grains feels like too much right now — from low appetite, nausea, or fatigue — small amounts spread through the day, plus staying hydrated, still help. Each day below offers two options. Some days contrast a vegetarian and a non-vegetarian dish; other days offer two different vegetarian dishes. Choose whichever suits you today — there''s no ''right'' path between A and B. If you are managing a health condition - Diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: Fibre-containing meals may support a steadier glucose response, but portions and pairings still matter. Follow your prescribed plan for foods such as rice, roti, porridge, bananas, dried fruit, and prunes, and increase fibre gradually as suggested above. - Thyroid condition: A major change in your usual fibre intake may affect how levothyroxine is absorbed. Take your medicine consistently as prescribed, and tell your clinician if your diet becomes substantially higher in fibre. Fibre supplements may need to be taken several hours apart from levothyroxine — follow your prescription or pharmacist''s timing advice. - High blood pressure or previous pre-eclampsia: The same gentle constipation guidance applies to you. Continue your prescribed medicines and monitoring. If constipation persists, ask your clinician or pharmacist which stool softener, laxative, or fibre supplement is suitable for you. How to use this week''s chart: These meals are a pool of gentle options, not a target to fit into one day. If you''re increasing fibre from a lower usual intake, choose one or two of the fibre-focused suggestions below and keep your other meals familiar — add more only as your digestion feels comfortable.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week is about keeping things moving gently — not with anything forceful, but with fibre and fluid that make bowel movements more comfortable. If you''re dealing with stitches, soreness, or haemorrhoids, this is directly for you.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with stewed or grated apple; add a small spoon of ground flaxseed only if it already suits your digestion.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-egg bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with baingan (brinjal) and moong dal sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable daliya (broken wheat) bowl, cooked through.", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe papaya bowl.", "nourishment_break_b": "A pear with a small handful of walnuts.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-barley soup with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "dinner_b": "Fish-and-vegetable stew (lower-mercury fish, cooked through) with soft rice.", "still_hungry": "Whatever feels easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why this happens right now", "notes": "Several ordinary postpartum things can still slow digestion at this stage: an iron supplement, if one has been prescribed; certain pain medicines, particularly opioid medicines; hormonal shifts; moving around less than usual; and simply not drinking enough. None of this means something is wrong with you — it''s a common, temporary combination, and food is one of the more comfortable ways to work with it.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain porridge (oats, barley, wheat flakes) with milk.", "breakfast_b": "Besan (chickpea flour) and vegetable chilla with curd.", "lunch_a": "Lobia (black-eyed pea) curry with brown rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Lower-mercury fish curry (cooked through) with soft rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava, well-washed.", "nourishment_break_b": "Two or three stewed prunes, increasing only if comfortable.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-paneer stir-fry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (cooked through) with soft rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fibre, gradually", "notes": "Adding a lot of fibre very suddenly can cause bloating, gas, or cramping — increasing it gradually, alongside plenty of fluids, works better and feels more comfortable. Today is a reminder to notice your own tolerance: if cabbage, apple skin, seeds, or whole grains make you feel more bloated, choose a softer, familiar alternative and return to them gradually.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa with a tomato chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich on whole-grain bread, made with fully cooked egg.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with cabbage sabzi and a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "Chia pudding made with fully hydrated chia seeds and pasteurized milk, kept refrigerated.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi (rice and masoor dal) with curd.", "dinner_b": "Soy-and-vegetable curry with soft rice.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Making bowel movements feel easier", "notes": "By now, bowel movements while stitches, soreness, or haemorrhoids are still healing may still feel uncomfortable. It is very unlikely that an ordinary bowel movement will open your stitches. Keeping stool soft can make passing it more comfortable and reduce the need to strain. If you had a third- or fourth-degree tear, continue the bowel-care plan and any medicines your maternity team prescribed. A low stool or footrest under your feet while sitting on the toilet, bringing your knees above your hips, can make passing stool easier and require less effort. Keep your abdomen relaxed, breathe out gently, and avoid holding your breath or forcing — a small, practical combination worth trying. Contact your care team if: constipation is not improving, bowel movements remain very painful, you repeatedly need to strain, haemorrhoids are worsening, you notice persistent blood in the stool, or stool is leaking or you cannot control bowel movements. This deserves assessment and shouldn''t be hidden through embarrassment. Seek urgent medical advice if: you have severe or worsening abdominal pain, repeated vomiting, a swollen abdomen, cannot pass stool or gas, have heavy rectal bleeding, or develop black, sticky, tar-like stool — especially with weakness, dizziness, or abdominal pain. Iron tablets can normally darken stool; tar-like stool or other concerning symptoms shouldn''t be assumed to be from iron.", "breakfast_a": "Whole-grain toast with peanut butter and a pear.", "breakfast_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable poha.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable-and-masoor-dal soup with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "One or two dried figs (anjeer), soaked overnight in clean water, with a few almonds.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of roasted flaxseed.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-mixed-bean stew with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-vegetable curry (cooked through) with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Foods that help, without overdoing it", "notes": "Cooked vegetables, ripe fruit, oats, and familiar grains may feel gentler for many mothers, though individual tolerance varies. You don''t need the roughest, highest-fibre food you can find — steady and well-tolerated works better than intense.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-and-oats khichdi with a spoon of ghee.", "breakfast_b": "Sattu (roasted gram) paratha with curd.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with pumpkin and chana (chickpea) sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-lentil stew (cooked through) with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "nourishment_break_a": "Stewed apple with cinnamon.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with sliced banana.", "dinner_a": "Curd rice with a cooked vegetable side.", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji with whole-wheat roti and a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Grab-and-go, gentle on digestion", "notes": "On a day when cooking isn''t happening, whole fruit, a handful of nuts, or something simple with oats can still support gentle digestion — you don''t need an elaborate meal for this to work. Food safety: Keep a prepared wrap or muffin refrigerated until you need it, and discard one that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Reheat the soup until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "Overnight oats made with pasteurized milk (refrigerated), topped with chopped guava, pear, apple, or another well-washed seasonal fruit (or berries, where available).", "breakfast_b": "Pre-made vegetable-and-egg muffins (mini frittata), kept refrigerated, with a piece of fruit.", "lunch_a": "A pre-made vegetable-and-hummus wrap, kept refrigerated until needed.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken-and-vegetable wrap, made with fully cooked chicken and kept refrigerated until needed.", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana and a small handful of walnuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small portion of raisins with a few cashews, following any glucose-management plan.", "dinner_a": "A reheated bowl of vegetable-and-lentil soup with toast or soft rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated bowl of soy-and-vegetable soup with toast or soft rice.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "This wasn''t the most glamorous week to talk about, but it''s one of the most common, real parts of early recovery — worth a quiet, judgment-free look back. Something as ordinary as fibre and fluid may have been supporting more comfortable healing this week.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Did anything this week make bathroom trips feel a little less worrying? What would you want to keep doing?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday part of eating well after birth.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 5: Vitamin C, for the healing still underway
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   5,
   NULL,
   'Phase 1 of 6 — Early healing',
   'Vitamin C, for the healing still underway',
   '"Healing isn''t finished when the stitches dissolve."',
   'If you have a C-section incision or a stitched perineal tear, your body''s repair work isn''t finished when the wound looks closed on the outside — tissue continues reorganizing and strengthening for weeks, sometimes months, after birth. Vitamin C supports normal collagen formation as part of an adequately nourishing diet — it works alongside protein, energy, zinc, iron, and appropriate wound care, not as a treatment by itself. It also helps your body absorb iron from plant foods more effectively, a useful pairing with Week 1''s focus on iron. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Meat, fish, and eggs generally provide little or no vitamin C — fruits and vegetables are the main practical sources in this week''s meals. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs, they''re still valuable for the protein side of healing (Week 3''s focus). A gentle note for this week: Most mothers can get enough vitamin C from varied fruits and vegetables. Don''t add a separate high-dose vitamin C supplement simply for faster healing — if your clinician has prescribed one, or your intake has been very limited, follow your individual plan instead of this general guidance. Vitamin C is sensitive to heat and dissolves into cooking water, so lightly cooked or fresh vegetables and fruits eaten close to preparation retain more of it than food that''s been boiled a long time or reheated repeatedly. If a full plate feels like too much: If fresh fruit or vegetables feel unappealing right now — from low appetite, nausea, or fatigue — even small amounts spread through the day still help. This is a nutrient where a little goes a long way. Each day below offers two options. Some days contrast a vegetarian and a non-vegetarian dish; other days offer two different vegetarian dishes. Choose whichever suits you today — there''s no ''right'' path between A and B. If you are managing a health condition - Diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: Whole fruits and vegetables can fit your prescribed plan, but portions and pairings still matter. Sweetened products such as amla murabba or amla candy aren''t equivalent to fresh amla — choose an unsweetened fruit option, or follow your individual glucose-management plan. - Thyroid condition: There''s no known concern between ordinary vitamin C-rich foods and levothyroxine. Continue taking your medicine consistently as prescribed. - Kidney disease or a history of kidney stones: Vitamin C from ordinary foods is generally appropriate, but don''t start a high-dose supplement without checking with your clinician first. Continue any kidney-specific dietary or fluid guidance you''ve already been given. - High blood pressure or previous pre-eclampsia: The food-based suggestions this week don''t need a special vitamin C adjustment. Continue your prescribed medicines and monitoring.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week is about the healing that''s still happening beneath the surface — vitamin C is one of several helpers in that process.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-and-capsicum stuffed paratha with curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable poha with a wedge of mosambi (sweet lime).", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with cauliflower-and-pea sabzi, a squeeze of lemon added after cooking.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable rice bowl (cooked through), with cauliflower and peas.", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange or mosambi (sweet lime).", "nourishment_break_b": "Fresh amla, if available, or a guava. (If amla murabba is what''s on hand, one small piece is fine as an occasional sweet food — not a substitute for fresh fruit.)", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-drumstick (moringa) leaves dal with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Lower-mercury fish-and-vegetable curry (cooked through) with soft rice.", "still_hungry": "Whatever feels easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Healing continues beneath the surface", "notes": "Wound healing happens in overlapping stages. The earliest inflammatory and tissue-building work begins soon after birth. By Week 5, an uncomplicated wound is often moving into the longer remodelling stage, when collagen is reorganized and the tissue gradually becomes stronger — a process that can continue for months. Adequate vitamin C remains one part of the varied nutrition your body uses during this ongoing recovery.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with milk and a small bowl of sliced guava.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable omelette (fully cooked) with toast and a small bowl of sliced guava.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable-and-drumstick sambar with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a cooked vegetable side.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of strawberries or sliced kiwi, where available, or a wedge of pineapple.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with an orange.", "dinner_a": "Cabbage sabzi with soft roti and a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-cabbage stir-fry with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "The collagen connection", "notes": "Your body uses amino acids from protein to make collagen, and vitamin C supports specific steps in that collagen-forming process. Both matter, but neither works alone — this is the same collagen-building process Week 3 introduced; today''s meals lean on both.", "breakfast_a": "Besan (chickpea flour) and tomato chilla with a mint-coriander chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach sandwich on whole-grain bread, with a piece of fruit (orange or guava).", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with capsicum-and-potato sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a squeeze of lemon.", "nourishment_break_a": "A few raw mango slices in season, plain or with a flavouring that suits your individual plan — or an orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with guava, orange segments, or strawberries where available.", "dinner_a": "Moong dal with mixed vegetables and soft roti, a squeeze of lemon added after cooking.", "dinner_b": "Fish-and-vegetable stew (lower-mercury fish, cooked through) with soft rice.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Getting the most from your food", "notes": "Vitamin C doesn''t hold up well to long cooking — it''s sensitive to heat and dissolves into cooking water. Cook vegetables until safely prepared and pleasantly tender; avoid unnecessarily prolonged boiling or repeated reheating, and eating some sources fresh (citrus, guava, amla, tomato) helps retain more too. Meat, eggs, and fish still need to be cooked all the way through regardless. Contact your care team promptly if: a C-section or perineal wound becomes increasingly red, hot, swollen, or painful; opens; develops pus, fluid, or an unpleasant smell; or you feel feverish or generally unwell. Food supports ordinary nutrition but can''t treat an infection or wound breakdown. Worth knowing: ''Pleasantly tender'' is about the vegetables — chicken, egg, and fish still need to be cooked all the way through, every time.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with diced papaya and a squeeze of lemon.", "breakfast_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable poha with a squeeze of lemon.", "lunch_a": "Cabbage-and-cauliflower stir-fry with whole-wheat roti.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable stir-fry (cooked through, vegetables cooked until tender) with whole-wheat roti.", "nourishment_break_a": "A whole orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "A thoroughly washed, freshly prepared cucumber-and-tomato salad with a squeeze of lemon and roasted chana.", "dinner_a": "Spinach moong dal with soft roti, a squeeze of lemon added after cooking.", "dinner_b": "Soy-and-mixed-vegetable curry (peas, carrots, green beans) with soft roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "On a day when cooking isn''t happening, whole fruit and simple, portable choices still count toward this week''s focus. Food safety: Keep the rice box refrigerated until you need it, and discard it if it''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Reheat the soup until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "A citrus fruit (orange or mosambi) with a small bowl of curd, a handful of nuts, and a slice of toast or small roti.", "breakfast_b": "A fully cooked boiled egg with toast and sliced guava.", "lunch_a": "Idli with a vegetable-and-tomato chutney and a piece of fruit (orange or guava).", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken-and-vegetable rice box, made with fully cooked chicken, kept refrigerated until needed.", "nourishment_break_a": "Fresh amla, if available, or a guava.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted peanuts with an orange.", "dinner_a": "A reheated bowl of vegetable-and-lentil soup with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "dinner_b": "A reheated bowl of paneer-and-vegetable curry with a slice of whole-grain bread.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A plant-only day, and that''s plenty", "notes": "Most practical dietary sources of vitamin C are plant foods — today''s choices are both vegetarian, because fruits and vegetables can cover this nutrient without needing meat, fish, or eggs.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-and-broccoli (or cauliflower, if broccoli isn''t available) dalia (broken wheat) porridge with a wedge of lemon.", "breakfast_b": "Stuffed cauliflower-and-pea paratha with curd.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a mixed-vegetable curry (cabbage, peas, carrot), a squeeze of lemon added after cooking.", "lunch_b": "Rajma (kidney bean) curry with rice and a cucumber-and-capsicum salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of pomegranate and guava.", "nourishment_break_b": "Fresh amla, if available, or a small bowl of curd with sliced kiwi (where available) or orange segments.", "dinner_a": "Broccoli (or cauliflower) and potato sabzi with khichdi (rice and moong dal).", "dinner_b": "Soy-and-mixed-vegetable curry with soft rice.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quieter nutrient, but a real one — worth a moment''s notice before the week closes. Your body has been continuing its repair work this week, mostly out of sight. Varied, adequately nourishing food is one way of supporting that ongoing recovery.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Did any of this week''s meals feel like a small, easy way to support your own healing?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday part of eating well after birth.')
