-- postpartum week 6: Zinc, for healing and immunity
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   6,
   NULL,
   'Phase 1 of 6 — Early healing',
   'Zinc, for healing and immunity',
   '"A small mineral, doing quiet, steady work."',
   'Zinc supports normal immune function, protein and DNA synthesis, cell division, and ordinary wound healing. It complements last week''s vitamin-C focus, although the two nutrients perform different jobs. If you eat meat, fish, or eggs: Zinc from animal foods is generally absorbed more readily. Meat and shellfish are among the richest sources, while fish, eggs, and dairy contribute varying amounts. Legumes, whole grains, nuts, and seeds are useful plant sources too. If you''re vegetarian: Soaking beans, grains, or seeds before cooking, and using fermented foods such as idli or dosa batter, can reduce some phytates and may improve zinc availability — the effect varies by food and method. Sprouted legumes should still be cooked thoroughly before eating. A gentle note for this week: Most mothers shouldn''t add a standalone zinc supplement solely for immunity or faster healing. If one has been prescribed, or your clinician has identified low intake or deficiency, follow that individual plan, and check the total zinc across all your supplements before adding another product. A separate high-dose iron supplement can reduce zinc absorption when both are taken at the same time — if you''ve been prescribed separate iron and zinc products, follow the label, pharmacist, or clinician''s timing instructions; a combined prenatal or postnatal formulation should just be taken as directed. This doesn''t mean iron- and zinc-containing foods need to be separated. Sustained high-dose zinc supplementation, well beyond ordinary food amounts, can interfere with how your body uses copper over time. If a full plate feels like too much: If a full meal feels like too much, smaller zinc-containing choices can still contribute — curd, roasted chana, a cooked egg, hummus, nut or seed paste, or a small portion of dal. Choose an option that''s easy to eat and appropriate for your individual health needs. Each day below offers two options. Some days contrast a vegetarian and a non-vegetarian dish; other days offer two different vegetarian dishes. Choose whichever suits you today — there''s no ''right'' path between A and B. If you are managing a health condition - Diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: Nuts, seeds, and legumes can fit your prescribed plan, but portions still matter. Til-gur chikki and laddus are sweet foods, not interchangeable with plain sesame or pumpkin seeds. - Kidney disease: Nuts, seeds, legumes, and dairy may need adjustment depending on your potassium, phosphorus, protein, or fluid instructions — follow your renal plan rather than increasing these automatically. - High blood pressure or previous pre-eclampsia: Choose unsalted nuts and seeds, and be mindful of salt in packaged sandwiches, wraps, rolls, and roasted snacks. - Thyroid condition: Continue levothyroxine exactly as prescribed. Mineral supplements can require particular timing, so check with your pharmacist before adding a standalone zinc supplement, rather than assuming the same schedule used for iron or calcium. Ordinary zinc-containing foods don''t need to be avoided.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This is the last week of Early healing''s focused nutrient series — zinc rounds out the healing and immune-support thread that vitamin C started last week.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-and-sesame stuffed paratha with curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with chana (chickpea) and spinach sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema with peas and whole-wheat roti (cooked through).", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted unsalted pumpkin seeds with an orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "A handful of unsalted cashews with a piece of fruit.", "dinner_a": "Sprouted moong-and-matki curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-sesame rice bowl.", "still_hungry": "Whatever feels easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Immunity, gently explained", "notes": "Your immune system relies on many factors working together — zinc is one contributor among several, alongside overall nourishment, protein, and whatever sleep and recovery are possible right now. Feeling run-down more easily right now doesn''t mean you''re doing anything wrong.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with milk and a spoon of roasted sesame seeds.", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable omelette (fully cooked) with toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a mixed-dal (masoor-toor) and vegetable curry.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-sesame millet bowl (cooked through).", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted unsalted chana with a few unsalted cashews.", "dinner_a": "Palak (spinach) and chana dal curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-chickpea bowl, sesame-tempered.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where zinc naturally shows up", "notes": "Zinc''s most concentrated sources are meat and shellfish; fish, eggs, and dairy contribute varying amounts. Pumpkin seeds, sesame seeds, cashews, legumes, and whole grains carry meaningful amounts too, just less easily absorbed.", "breakfast_a": "Bajra (pearl millet)-and-sesame vegetable cheela with a mint-coriander chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich on whole-grain bread.", "lunch_a": "Rajma (kidney bean)-and-millet bowl with a cooked vegetable side.", "lunch_b": "Baked or pan-cooked lower-mercury fish with a cooked vegetable side and rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with roasted sesame seeds.", "nourishment_break_b": "A handful of roasted unsalted peanuts with a guava.", "dinner_a": "Mixed-vegetable-and-whole-wheat dalia khichdi with curd.", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-sesame noodles (cooked through) with a cooked vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Making plant sources work well", "notes": "Zinc is genuinely harder to get from plant foods alone, but preparation methods like soaking, sprouting, and fermenting can help. Today''s choices are both vegetarian.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with sambar.", "breakfast_b": "Fermented dosa with a paneer-and-vegetable filling.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with sprouted matki (moth bean) curry.", "lunch_b": "Dahi chana chaat, made with safely cooked chickpeas, roasted cumin, and sesame.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted unsalted pumpkin seeds and cashews.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with sliced banana.", "dinner_a": "Chana (chickpea) and spinach curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Thick lentil dal dip with idli or whole-wheat toast, with vegetable sticks on the side if desired.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "On a day when cooking isn''t happening, a handful of unsalted seeds or nuts is a genuinely useful, portable way to keep this week''s focus going. Food safety: Keep the roll, dosa, or frittata muffins refrigerated until you need them, and discard anything that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Reheat the dal-and-dalia bowl or curry until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "A citrus fruit with a small bowl of curd and roasted unsalted pumpkin seeds.", "breakfast_b": "A fully cooked egg-and-vegetable frittata muffin (made ahead), with a piece of fruit.", "lunch_a": "Dosa with an unsweetened peanut-and-sesame chutney, kept portable, and a piece of fruit.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken-and-sesame roll, made with fully cooked chicken, kept refrigerated until needed.", "nourishment_break_a": "A handful of unsalted almonds or walnuts with a banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "A handful of roasted unsalted pumpkin seeds, or one small piece of til-gur chikki (sesame-jaggery brittle) as an occasional sweet.", "dinner_a": "A reheated thick moong-dal-and-vegetable dalia (broken wheat) bowl.", "dinner_b": "A reheated rajma-and-vegetable rice bowl with a spoon of curd.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Rounding out Early healing", "notes": "This is the last themed day before Early healing closes — a reminder that no single nutrient did the work alone these past six weeks. Iron, protein, fibre, vitamin C, and zinc all played their part, alongside rest, movement, and time.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with roasted unsalted peanuts and a squeeze of lemon.", "breakfast_b": "Besan-and-vegetable chilla with a fully cooked egg on the side.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with lobia (black-eyed pea) curry.", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable pulao (cooked through).", "nourishment_break_a": "A handful of unsalted peanuts with an orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted unsalted pumpkin seeds, or one small piece of til (sesame) laddu as an occasional sweet.", "dinner_a": "Mixed-vegetable-and-chana dal curry with soft roti.", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peanut vegetable rice bowl with curd.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "Six weeks of small, steady choices — worth a quiet look back before this phase closes. Six weeks of Early healing are complete. Iron, energy, protein, fibre, vitamin C, and zinc all had their turn — ordinary food, doing quiet, steady work the whole time.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Looking back at six weeks of Early healing, what''s one thing about how you''ve been eating that you''d want to keep?',
   'This closes Early healing — next week begins Finding rhythm, the next stretch of this journey.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
